> Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or to their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We ask where the value of the reasoning tokens goes in that decision. Six small ($\le$14B) open models choose among 5 or 7 bids with empirical win curves from eBay prices, under forced budgets of up to 768 tokens, and are scored on held-out auctions against an exact calculator. They recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead. Arithmetic is a large part of the remaining loss for one model only. In 62--93\% of it the trace had already written the best option's expected profit as the largest, and the answer chose another bid. Part of this comes from answering before the list is finished. The wrong bids are not random: four models pick the highest bid, the one most likely to win, at more than twice the chance rate, which under pay-your-bid pricing overpays the seller; the other two follow list position, a pattern that replicates on synthetic auctions. Sending answers that disagree with the trace's own numbers to a calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap while calling it in 18--57\% of decisions.
