< We use 628 eBay auctions of Palm Pilot PDAs, Xbox consoles and Cartier watches \cite{jank2010modeling,onlineauctionsdataset}, split 70/30 within product. Per product, 18 private values $v$ are spaced evenly from the 35th percentile of construction-sample closing prices to 1.25 times the 95th. For each value and menu size $K\in\{5,7\}$, two candidate sets of \$5-step bids are drawn below $v$ within the 2nd--98th price percentiles, as a random contiguous window or a random sample, never conditioned on the optimum: 216 scenarios. Each bid shows $\hat p(b)$, the share of construction prices at or below $b$; the chosen bid is scored with the same estimator on 189 held-out auctions. eBay prices come from proxy bidding, so these are realistic empirical win curves rather than first-price win rates; models and calculator see the same landscape.
