< \paragraph{Wrong answers overbid.}
< At 768 tokens, 81--91\% of the wrong answers of DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models bid above the calculator, by 12--20\% of the private value on average; for Phi-mini and Granite-8B the figure is 60\% and 51\%. Averaged over all decisions, expected payment to the seller is \$18--\$83 above the calculator's choice while the win probability differs by at most 4 points, so most of the lost profit is paid to sellers rather than buying a better chance of winning. Lost expected profit is 2.0--5.2\% of the private value for Cartier, 0.3--3.8\% for Xbox and at most 0.8\% for Palm.
---
> \paragraph{What the wrong bids cost.}
> Among wrong answers, 81--91\% of those of DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models bid above the calculator, against 55--61\% if they were drawn at random from the same menus; Phi-mini (60\% vs 51\%) and Granite-8B (51\% vs 46\%) are close to chance. Under the pay-your-bid objective, these wrong answers raise the chance of winning by 8--14 points but raise the expected payment from \$239--\$339 to \$459--\$717 per decision, so they buy probability at a loss of \$94--\$164 of expected profit each. The tilt toward high bids holds when the choices are re-scored: 47--65\% of wrong answers overbid under the lognormal curve and 73--89\% under shaded prices. Lost expected profit over all decisions is 2.0--5.2\% of the private value for Cartier, 0.3--3.8\% for Xbox and at most 0.8\% for Palm.
