< Taking the option with the largest value the trace wrote, once two values exist, recovers 73--96\% of the gap at 768 tokens and 65--88\% at 512, where the answer recovers 17--56\%; it never lowers $q$ by more than 0.026 at any budget, and changes by at most 4 points for segment windows of 300--1{,}000 characters (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c). Waiting for $K-1$ values gives 73--92\%. The oracle reader, which uses true values and acts on any evaluated option, falls below the answer at 256 tokens and bounds the reader only once most options are evaluated. On the synthetic traces, which we did not use to design the reader, it helps Phi-mini and Granite-8B (47\% to 86\%, 65\% to 73\%) but lowers recovery by 3--14 points for the other four, whose answers already track their traces. The reader is therefore better as an audit than as a replacement: it disagrees with the answer in 18--57\% of 768-token decisions, and the answer is wrong in 58--89\% of those. Where the agent can call a calculator, the calculator is better still; the audit applies where a platform or user can read an agent's trace but not change its tools.
