< \paragraph{Routing and costs.}
< A gradient-boosted router, trained with nested cross-validation grouped by value family, beats a random mix of 0 and 768 tokens by 0.012--0.031 in $q$ at equal spend within known products, but we cannot show that it transfers to a new product (areas 0.31--0.58, random 0.50). Tokens are cheap: 768 tokens pay over budget zero once a decision's best expected profit exceeds a few cents (Table~\ref{tab:main}), and below \$0.35 even at \$50 per million tokens, the top of current API list prices \cite{openai2026pricing,anthropic2026pricing,google2026pricing,deepseek2026pricing}. The cost that counts is the value left behind, \$15--\$36 of expected profit per decision, mostly on Cartier.
