< Our objective is the pay-your-bid rule of first-price ad exchanges \cite{despotakis2021firstprice}, used as motivation rather than tested: eBay runs proxy bidding, under which bidding one's value is optimal \cite{roth2002lastminute}, and we use eBay only for realistic win curves. The calculator is an oracle; we do not test a model that calls a tool and can fail to. The synthetic replication recovers more (47--98\%) because its menus spread expected profits more widely; adding that spread to a pooled regression of the per-decision gain cuts the synthetic advantage from 0.088 ($p<0.001$) to 0.030 ($p=0.38$), though a remainder cannot be ruled out. Larger models, longer budgets, prompting and other mechanisms are untested.
