> For $\le$14B open reasoning models making a single-bidder bid-landscape decision under budgets up to 768 forced tokens, arithmetic costs money for one model only. The traces usually write down the right comparison; the loss comes when the answer is chosen, partly from answering before the list is finished, and the wrong answers are not random: four models drift to the bid most likely to win, the others to list positions. Under pay-your-bid pricing that drift overpays sellers. An audit that compares the answer with the trace's own numbers and calls a calculator when they disagree recovers most of the loss.
